{"assets":["/editorial-assets/04be10b2c40c61b6c58ef4cf1657164a9b6c6b009f412415d2ab49f2acf75437.png","/editorial-assets/34ca8ce676cb28dea5931e330716faf7f76aa49a4b70d86a9c79ecba89e1575f.png","/editorial-assets/3fba37ce2ea90d4a67e35febee4c2834f4d1f90f5212bdbd337d5acbb35be43b.png","/editorial-assets/b7e0a7402539f848b514b627b64e62184fefb2d9d5adac6f6019aa54f011a4e4.png","/editorial-assets/73542cd31a555ca585121274e2f255e46c92d784108f46e67b4d846dec9b1786.png","/editorial-assets/1bd2af03a068872c00707687a2ca748ce49c5e257027c009bf5f511b9a19748d.png","/editorial-assets/4e6cd847b91dbcfd9e76489936b3a770cbeac9a865185c2070583a19d3977c8a.png","/editorial-assets/552af358f2f051d8e3332471f6b23f7a8df581fb431459ee1c2f3323a518acbc.png","/editorial-assets/062a245714a873cf717d66b6e8b3efb203f032d05189a884327b05115749106b.png","/editorial-assets/5e27bba493890abcd889e0a813a56918929ad1db345aa938bd1e6c804229f8e8.png","/editorial-assets/8a87f377533e9b96d9e69fa5c614571ed25aef20416b7e938c2dded50ee2f0d2.png","/editorial-assets/096355ed3557dbb012fc77f8045118f6fcb4d498de5cc88ee68a62325e5e0f6f.png","/editorial-assets/aa57906cdabf6fd3bfeacde4efbe2b9f0b58211c58f73c59fcc6342d48a738bf.png","/editorial-assets/765dd7c9f4ed319269abb75c132c41a14ae32fdff5fdd23f3f60422e5cdf21f8.png","/editorial-assets/310166033646ce6deba8aa3544a95cfcd6a66d99f72f67c6a099dba1e1ed5faf.png","/editorial-assets/1b62637ce52f9d68d6b36c33bc36fa08614b3afe0e0701848813ec122c5a6009.png"],"contestId":"859","diagnostics":[],"document":{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"MemSQL Start[c]UP 3.0 Round 1 is over!"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Congratulations to "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/EvenImage"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"EvenImage"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/Petr"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Petr"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/eatmore"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"eatmore"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/tourist"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"tourist"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":" on solving all the problems!"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Here are our solutions: "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"859A"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/859/problem/A"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"859A - Declined Finalists"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"If all ranks are 25 or less, then the answer is 0, since it's possible that all of the top 25 accepted the invitation. Otherwise, notice that the highest invited rank is initially 25, and increases by 1 every time a contestant declines. Therefore the answer is simply the highest rank minus 25."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"859B"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/859/problem/B"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"859B - Lazy Security Guard"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's ask the problem a different way. For a fixed perimeter, what's the maximum number of city blocks we can fit in it? The maximum will always be achieved by a rectangle, because that's the only shape that doesn't contain any "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/04be10b2c40c61b6c58ef4cf1657164a9b6c6b009f412415d2ab49f2acf75437.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" angles. A "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/04be10b2c40c61b6c58ef4cf1657164a9b6c6b009f412415d2ab49f2acf75437.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" angle is not allowed because there would be a way to add another city block without affecting the perimeter. The problem is therefore equivalent to finding the minimum-perimeter rectangle with area at least "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"N"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"A "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"H"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" × "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"W"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" rectangle has a perimeter of 2·("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"H"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"W"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"). The constraints of the problem were low enough that one could loop through every possible value of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"H"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and find the smallest corresponding "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"W"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" such that "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"H"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"·"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"W"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≥ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"N"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". We can show that the best rectangle will always have "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/34ca8ce676cb28dea5931e330716faf7f76aa49a4b70d86a9c79ecba89e1575f.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":". Consider what happens if "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"W"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≥ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"H"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 2. Then ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"W"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1)·("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"H"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1) = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"W"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"·"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"H"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"W"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"H"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") - 1 ≥ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"W"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"·"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"H"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + (2) - 1 = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"W"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"·"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"H"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1. In other words, the ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"W"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1) × ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"H"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1) rectangle has greater area than the "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"W"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" × "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"H"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" rectangle, but the same perimeter."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"A direct solution therefore sets "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/3fba37ce2ea90d4a67e35febee4c2834f4d1f90f5212bdbd337d5acbb35be43b.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"H"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = ⌈ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"N"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" / "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"W"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"⌉."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"859C"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/859/problem/C"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"859C - Pie Rules"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Denote "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Score"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"L"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") as the total sizes of slices that will be eaten by whoever holds the decider token, for a list of pies "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"L"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", and denote "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Total"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"L"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") as the total size of all slices, and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Rest"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"L"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") as the list of pies formed by removing the first pie. Note that the total sizes of slices eaten by whoever doesn't hold the decider token is given by "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Total"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"L"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Score"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"L"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's consider the options available to the participant with the decider token. If they choose to take the pie for themselves, they end up with "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"L"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"[0] + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Total"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Rest"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"L"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")) - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Score"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Rest"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"L"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")) total pie. If they let the other participant have the slice, they end up with "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Score"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Rest"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"L"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")) total pie. They will choose whichever option is larger. To compute the answer, we simply start from the end of the game and work backwards to the beginning."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Challenge: Try to solve the variant of this game where instead of handing out slices in a fixed order, the participant who doesn't currently hold the decider token gets to select which slice is handed out next. The optimal strategy is not difficult to discover, but proving it is very difficult."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"859D"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/859/problem/D"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"859D - Third Month Insanity"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Denote "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"opp"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") as the set of teams that could play against team "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" in round "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"w"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"]["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"] is the probability that team "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" defeats team "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". We would first like to compute the probability that team "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" wins a game in round "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", and call this "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"]["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"]. Set "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"[0]["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"] = 1 for all teams for convenience. Then for "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≥ 1 we have that the probability "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" wins a game in round "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is equal to the probability that they won a game in round "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1, times the sum, over all opponents, of the probability of facing that opponent times the probability of defeating that opponent. In mathematical terms, "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/b7e0a7402539f848b514b627b64e62184fefb2d9d5adac6f6019aa54f011a4e4.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Now let us denote "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"S"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"]["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"] as the maximum expected score that can be achieved after "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" rounds, choosing team "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" as a winner in round "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", and only counting points scored in games played between teams that could play in the game that "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" would play in in round "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". In other words, consider the sub-bracket which contains "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" but only goes for "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" rounds. Our desired result is "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/73542cd31a555ca585121274e2f255e46c92d784108f46e67b4d846dec9b1786.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Begin by setting "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"S"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"[0]["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"] = 0 for all "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". Then for each round and team compute "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/1bd2af03a068872c00707687a2ca748ce49c5e257027c009bf5f511b9a19748d.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"859E"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/859/problem/E"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"859E - Desk Disorder"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Consider an undirected graph where the desks are vertices and the engineers are edges. For each engineer an edge connects their current desk to their desired desk. Each connected component of this graph can be assigned independently, with the final result being the product of the number of assignments over the individual components."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"What type of graphs are the connected components? We know because it's connected that "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"E"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≥ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"V"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1, where "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"E"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"V"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" are number of edges and vertices, respectively. Also note that "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"E"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"V"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" due to the condition that no two engineers currently sit at the same desk. It follows that there are only 2 cases to consider."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Case 1: "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"E"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"V"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1. In this case the component is a tree. We claim that the number of assignments is equal to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"V"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" in this case. To see why this is true, consider that after we choose which of the "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"V"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" desks to leave empty, there's always exactly 1 way to assign engineers to the remaining desks."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Case 2: "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"E"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"V"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". In this case the component has exactly one cycle. Engineers currently sitting at a desk on the cycle can either all stay at their current desk, or all move to their desired desk. Engineers currently sitting at a desk not on the cycle must remain at their current desks. Therefore there will be exactly 2 assignments, unless the cycle was a self-loop (an engineer whose desired desk is equal to their current desk), in which case there is only 1 assignment."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"To compute the result, we can use a "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Disjoint-set_data_structure"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Disjoint-set data structure"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":". For each connected component, we keep track of its size and what type of cycle (if any) is present. Initially each desk is in a component by itself. For each engineer, we merge the components containing their current and desired desks, or mark the component as containing a cycle if the current and desired desks were already in the same component. Finally we multiply together the numbers of assignments of the components."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"859F"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/859/problem/F"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"859F - Ordering T-Shirts"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let us first describe how to tell if a given set of T-shirts is sufficient to award the competitors. For any set of T-shirt sizes, it clearly must be the case that the total number of T-shirts ordered of sizes in the set must be at least as large as the maximum number of competitors that could require a T-shirt size from the set. We claim that this is sufficient as well, and will prove it using the max-flow min-cut theorem."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Construct a flow graph with a source, one node for each type of survey response, one node for each T-shirt size, and a sink. Add an edge from the source to each survey response node with capacity equal to the number of winners with that response. Add an edge from each survey response to the corresponding T-shirt size(s) with unlimited capacity. Add an edge from each T-shirt size to the sink with capacity equal to the number of T-shirts ordered. Find a minimum cut in this graph. Then for any maximal contiguous range of T-shirt sizes in the cut, we can remove those T-shirt sizes from the cut and instead cut the corresponding survey responses. It follows that the cut consisting only of survey responses is minimal, and by the max-flow min-cut theorem, an assignment exists that awards all winners T-shirts."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Note that we don't need to consider all sets of T-shirt sizes - only contiguous sets of T-shirts sizes are relevant. If a non-contiguous set of T-shirt sizes violates the constraint, then one of its contiguous subsections must also violate the constraint."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"In other words, if we order "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" shirts of size "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", we only need to satisfy "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/4e6cd847b91dbcfd9e76489936b3a770cbeac9a865185c2070583a19d3977c8a.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We now will show that the algorithm that greedily orders as few of each T-shirt as possible, starting from the smallest size, produces an optimal result. Suppose, to the contrary, that a better solution exists, and consider the lexicographically smallest such solution. At the first point where the solutions differ, the optimal solution must order more T-shirts (due to the greedy nature of the algorithm). If we change the optimal solution by ordering one fewer of that size and one more of the next size up, the solution will remain valid. However this violates the lexicographical minimality of the solution, a contradiction."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Our solution is for each index "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" from 1 to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", to set "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/552af358f2f051d8e3332471f6b23f7a8df581fb431459ee1c2f3323a518acbc.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":". This can easily be done in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":") time if we compute cumulative sums of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"In order to solve it faster, we need to be able to quickly find the index "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" that maximizes the above expression. First lets consider the indexes where "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" < "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" + ... + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":". Because all "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" terms are non-negative, we only need to consider the largest such "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" for which this is true, which can be found in amortized constant time."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Now consider indexes where "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≥ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" + ... + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":". Denote "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"]["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"] = ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind"